{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means fewer distractions—not fewer comforts.** In a small flat, it’s a complete, useful home with furniture and finishes that work calmly together.\n\n- **Purposeful furniture:** comfortable seating, a reachable table, lighting and enough storage—not an almost-empty room.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange pieces so moving between doors, windows and everyday activities feels easy.\n- **A restrained palette:** repeat a few colours and materials rather than making every piece a statement.\n- **Less visible clutter:** closed storage for everyday belongings, with a few favourite objects on display.\n- **Warmth through texture:** wood, woven fabric and a rug can keep simple shapes from feeling stark.\n\nFor your flat, the existing warm off-white walls are a good starting point. I’d suggest coordinated living and dining zones, with storage along solid walls and windows left unobstructed. That’s a design direction, not a change to your scene.", "conversationId": "312767fe971348a79802e77da4e674d1"}
